/**
 * Stroke-order data for the writing/tracing stage (BACKLOG.md's
 * 2026-09-08 entry), covering just the distinct hanzi that appear
 * across this project's own idiom set (idioms.ts) — not the full
 * hanzi-writer-data corpus (tens of thousands of characters), per the
 * backlog's own "stroke data bundled locally for just this project's
 * distinct characters (not the full CDN dataset)" call.
 *
 * Sourced from hanzi-writer-data 2.0.1
 * (https://github.com/chanind/hanzi-writer-data), itself derived from
 * the Make Me a Hanzi project. Distributed under the Arphic Public
 * License — see writingStrokeData.LICENSE.txt in this same directory
 * for the full license text, carried alongside this derived
 * (character-subset) copy per that license's redistribution terms.
 * `strokes` are SVG path `d` strings for each stroke in drawing
 * order; `medians` are each stroke's simplified point sequence, in
 * the same character-space coordinates (see Make Me a Hanzi's docs) —
 * both consumed directly by the hanzi-writer package's own
 * `charDataLoader` (writingStage.ts), not reprocessed here.
 *
 * Regenerate with (not part of the normal build — a one-off, rerun
 * only if idioms.ts's character set ever changes): download
 * hanzi-writer-data 2.0.1, pull just idioms.ts's distinct `hanzi`
 * characters' own `<char>.json` files, and re-emit this file mapping
 * each to its {strokes, medians, radStrokes} (dropping every other
 * field that package's data ships, which this project doesn't use).
 * 2026-09-10: regenerated after the idiom pool grew 15 -> 30 (this
 * file previously only covered the original 15's 51 characters,
 * silently missing the other 47 the growth introduced — caught by a
 * flaky "random idiom reroll" e2e test hanging on one of them).
 * 2026-09-12: regenerated again after the pool grew 45 -> 60 — this
 * time caught by the full e2e suite itself (not a flaky one-off):
 * today's date-seeded 3-idiom session drew a character introduced by
 * the 30->45 batch, which turned out to have *never* been added back
 * on 2026-09-10 either (that regeneration only targeted the 15->30
 * growth this same commit's diff was against, not the 30->45 batch
 * landed the same day) — 82 characters were missing in total, not
 * just this batch's own 15 idioms' new ones. Regenerated against the
 * full current 60-idiom set (180 distinct characters) so nothing is
 * silently missing going forward, and worth re-checking after every
 * future idiom-pool growth rather than assuming "just the new batch"
 * is the only gap.
 */

